  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Increased capacity of national tripartite constituents to design and implement a transformative agenda on gender equality, non-discrimination and inclusion</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Trinidad and Tobago has a national workplace response to HIV that aims to prevent discrimination and ensure workers living with HIV are treated fairly and with dignity. However, stigma and misinformation still affect behaviour in workplaces and can discourage people from seeking testing or support.
This initiative will help the Ministry of Labour and Small Enterprise Development&#x2019;s HIV Workplace Advocacy Unit (HWAU) and the social partners to put in place a practical, visible measure to reduce HIV-related stigma at work: an HIV Art Commission implemented in selected workplaces. Using a &#x201c;Social Art Design&#x201d; approach, artists will work with workers to explore attitudes and experiences through structured workplace discussions, and then translate the insights into artworks and messages that promote respect, inclusion, and non-discrimination.
The project will involve selecting and preparing artists, engaging approximately 12 workplaces, and producing artworks and communication materials that can be used for continued awareness and behaviour-change efforts. The resulting evidence, materials, and dissemination will strengthen national capacity to implement workplace measures that support equal opportunities and treatment for people living with HIV, in line with international labour standards and Sustainable Development Goals.</narrative>
  </description>
